riation  that you will see is this MS la  variation which is Pawnee 4 c 6 d 4 D 5  and when he brings his Knight to c3 and  we recapture then we bring our Knight to  d7 and the goal of this is to eventually  bring our Knight to f6 and once we  exchanged Knights then our B Knight our  Queen side Knight will be on f6 and then  we can open up our bishop here now white  can have a lot of play here if you just  play something like Knight to g5 and  this is actually what Kasparov lost to  when he played the con defense against  deep blue there's just a lot of traps  that you can fall into why it's not  going to allow black to exchange the  Knights here and so I feel like it's a  trickier position to play as black and I  usually don't recommend it to people  when they're learning the ComicCon but  it is good to know about if you do see  it another thing you may see and the  ComicCon starts out before we'll go over  some more variations here c6 d4 pawn d5  then he brings his Knight to c3 and  capture and recapture one things you may  see here is Knight to f6 and then when  recaptures there's two variations  there's a Bronstein Larson which is  simply pawn captures on f6 which the  goal of this is to give an open file to  the rook yeah it does give an open file  to the rook but it does kind of  undermine our pawn structure here it's  going to be real tough for us to Castle  kingside we're more than likely not  going to Castle kingside we want to keep  this open file for our route but in the  in game our pawn structure is not quite  going to be as good as wide as you can  see we have three pawn islands here it's  going to be a little harder for us other  variation you may see in this is  you may see pawn capture on f6 which I  feel is a little bit better  you can still Castle kingside and get a  lot of play but those are two of the  things you may see now another variation  that you will see is the advanced  variation starts out the same  efore c 6d 5d 4d 5 but instead of now  playing Knight to c3 white may simply  play pawn to e5 now from here black has  two options the first one is bringing  his Bishop to f5 where the knight can  now come to c3 and after we develop with  pawn to e6 very common move after we  bring our Bishop out to move pawn to e6  then Y can play pawn to g4 putting a lot  of pressure on our Bishop and we're  going to have to kick our Bishop back  out this is one thing you may see white  excuse me black does have another option  here if he doesn't want to play  f5 he can play c5 and from here if he  white chooses to take then we can play  e6 and it kind of takes on a French  defense here so we are giving up  material but we're looking to get it  back later on if you're familiar with  the French defense you'll definitely  want to take a look at this variation  it's something that you may see quite  often another variation that you may see  quite often if someone's not too  familiar with the main line is after c6  d4 and d5 it's not as common but you may  see white take on d5 and from here we  can take on d5 with our pawn this is  going to be completely fine with this  there's a lot of things that white can  do but as you can tell already we have  both of our Center pawns and white only  has one of the center pawns we can  simply bring our knight into action with  c6 putting a lot of pressure on the  center I feel like this is actually a  better position for black as we still  have both of our Center pawns and we can  develop like normal White's missing his  epon here and I feel like he's better  off playing c3 instead of taking in the  exchange variation so if you see this  this is actually very good for black you  can set  you can develop like normal and you're  going to have a very good game so these  are some of the common things you'll see  in the CARICOM hope you guys enjoyed if  you haven't already please subscribe and  I'll see you guys next video thanks for  watching

